WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.600
Third block.

2
00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:05.775
Everything so far has involved exactly two sequences.

3
00:00:05.775 --> 00:00:11.200
But a protein family has many members, and a family tells you things no pair can.

4
00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:16.775
The natural idea is to extend dynamic programming to more sequences.

5
00:00:16.775 --> 00:00:20.925
It does extend — correctly, provably — and it is useless.

6
00:00:20.925 --> 00:00:25.300
For N sequences the matrix needs one dimension per sequence.

7
00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:28.075
Length L to the power N cells.

8
00:00:28.075 --> 00:00:32.925
Two sequences of a hundred residues: ten thousand cells, trivial.

9
00:00:32.925 --> 00:00:37.700
Ten sequences of a hundred residues: ten to the twentieth cells.

10
00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:41.850
That is not a matter of waiting longer or buying a bigger machine.

11
00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:49.050
It is the same kind of wall as the brute-force count at the start of the session, and ten sequences is a small family.

12
00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:51.625
Real families have hundreds.

13
00:00:51.625 --> 00:00:58.025
So exact multiple alignment is off the table, and every tool you will ever use is a heuristic.

14
00:00:58.025 --> 00:01:06.675
It is worth being clear about what that costs: for two sequences we had a guarantee of optimality, and for many sequences we give it up.

15
00:01:06.675 --> 00:01:11.375
A multiple alignment is a good arrangement, not a provably best one.

16
00:01:11.975 --> 00:01:17.975
Practical methods combine three ideas, and knowing which is which tells you how a tool will fail.

17
00:01:17.975 --> 00:01:30.075
Progressive alignment computes all the pairwise distances, builds a guide tree from them, aligns the closest pair first, and then adds sequences or already-aligned groups one at a time, following the tree.

18
00:01:30.075 --> 00:01:35.225
It is fast and it is the classic approach — ClustalW is the example.

19
00:01:35.225 --> 00:01:39.950
Its weakness is structural: early decisions are never revisited.

20
00:01:39.950 --> 00:01:48.425
A gap placed wrongly in the first merge, when only two sequences were visible, stays there while every later sequence is aligned around it.

21
00:01:48.425 --> 00:01:52.050
Iterative refinement addresses exactly that.

22
00:01:52.050 --> 00:02:00.100
Start from a progressive alignment, then repeatedly pull parts of it apart and re-align them, keeping changes that improve the score.

23
00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:03.300
MUSCLE and MAFFT work this way.

24
00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:06.450
Early mistakes get a chance to be undone.

25
00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:12.175
Consistency uses the agreement among all the pairwise alignments as extra evidence.

26
00:02:12.175 --> 00:02:20.350
If A aligns with B at some position, and B aligns with C there, then a multiple alignment where A also matches C there is preferred.

27
00:02:20.350 --> 00:02:22.750
T-Coffee is the example.

28
00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:29.875
It is slower, and it is better on hard cases, because it uses information the progressive approach throws away.

29
00:02:29.875 --> 00:02:32.250
Real tools mix these.

30
00:02:32.250 --> 00:02:43.600
Which does best depends on how many sequences you have, how long they are, and how diverged they are — which is why benchmarks disagree and why the answer is to try more than one on your own data.

31
00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:49.475
The guide tree deserves a closer look, since it determines the order of every merge.

32
00:02:49.475 --> 00:02:54.200
It is built by ordinary hierarchical clustering on a distance matrix.

33
00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:57.475
Every sequence starts as its own cluster.

34
00:02:57.475 --> 00:03:00.850
Repeatedly merge the closest pair of clusters.

35
00:03:00.850 --> 00:03:03.550
Stop when one cluster remains.

36
00:03:03.550 --> 00:03:07.900
The merge order is the alignment order — that is the whole purpose.

37
00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:16.075
The worked example has five toy sequences, almost identical except for sequence four, with their pairwise Hamming distances.

38
00:03:16.075 --> 00:03:24.750
Follow the merges: the close ones pair up early, and sequence four joins only at the very last step, because it differs clearly from the other four.

39
00:03:24.750 --> 00:03:32.650
The tree has put the easy decisions first and the hard one last, which is exactly what you want when early decisions are permanent.

40
00:03:32.650 --> 00:03:37.500
Now the question to hold on to: this looks like a phylogenetic tree.

41
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:39.100
Is it one?

42
00:03:39.100 --> 00:03:45.000
It has leaves, branches, and a nested structure, and it was built from sequence distances.

43
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:48.325
Keep it in mind — we answer it on the next slide.

44
00:03:48.925 --> 00:03:57.225
The answer is no, and the distinction is worth being precise about, because the two kinds of tree look alike and answer different questions.

45
00:03:57.225 --> 00:04:01.250
A guide tree asks: in what order should I align these?

46
00:04:01.250 --> 00:04:11.325
It is built from quick pairwise distances, often before any alignment exists, usually by UPGMA, which assumes every lineage evolves at the same rate.

47
00:04:11.325 --> 00:04:16.450
It is a computational shortcut, and its branch lengths are not meant to be read.

48
00:04:16.450 --> 00:04:21.075
A phylogenetic tree asks: how are these related by descent?

49
00:04:21.075 --> 00:04:27.750
It is built from a multiple alignment with an evolutionary model, by neighbour joining or maximum likelihood.

50
00:04:27.750 --> 00:04:33.450
It is a hypothesis about evolution, and its branch lengths mean the amount of change.

51
00:04:33.450 --> 00:04:45.550
The consequence of UPGMA's equal-rates assumption is concrete: a fast-evolving sequence looks distant, so it gets placed as though it branched off early, when it may simply have been changing quickly.

52
00:04:45.550 --> 00:04:48.675
Good enough for deciding an alignment order.

53
00:04:48.675 --> 00:04:51.575
Not good enough for a claim about history.

54
00:04:51.575 --> 00:05:02.575
Neighbour joining uses the same pairwise distance matrix but allows lineages to evolve at different rates, which is why it is suitable for phylogeny where UPGMA is not.

55
00:05:02.575 --> 00:05:04.625
Reading a tree, then.

56
00:05:04.625 --> 00:05:07.550
Leaves are the sequences you put in.

57
00:05:07.550 --> 00:05:12.225
Internal nodes are inferred ancestors — inferred, not observed.

58
00:05:12.225 --> 00:05:17.150
Branch lengths are the amount of change, here the fraction of differing residues.

59
00:05:17.150 --> 00:05:21.200
A clade is an ancestor together with all of its descendants.

60
00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:24.075
And trees are rooted or unrooted.

61
00:05:24.075 --> 00:05:30.575
Only a rooted tree has a direction of time, so only a rooted tree lets you read off the order of events.

62
00:05:30.575 --> 00:05:33.775
Distance methods give unrooted trees.

63
00:05:33.775 --> 00:05:43.350
A root is added either with an outgroup — a sequence known to have branched off before all the others — or, as here, at the midpoint of the longest path through the tree.

64
00:05:43.350 --> 00:05:48.950
On this tree, red squares mark duplications and blue circles mark speciations.

65
00:05:48.950 --> 00:05:57.475
The two topmost splits are both duplications, and here is how you can tell: each branch below them still contains all three species.

66
00:05:57.475 --> 00:06:02.425
If the gene had been copied after the species diverged, that could not be true.

67
00:06:03.025 --> 00:06:09.050
Now we can settle the orthologs-and-paralogs question from the first block, with real numbers.

68
00:06:09.050 --> 00:06:14.950
Human alpha-globin against mouse alpha-globin: fourteen per cent of positions differ.

69
00:06:14.950 --> 00:06:18.550
Against chicken alpha-globin: thirty per cent.

70
00:06:18.550 --> 00:06:23.100
Both of those pairs are orthologs, separated by speciations.

71
00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:28.375
Human alpha-globin against human beta-globin: fifty-four per cent differ.

72
00:06:28.375 --> 00:06:34.425
Same genome, same cell, same protein complex — and far more different than the chicken gene.

73
00:06:34.425 --> 00:06:40.175
Those are paralogs, separated by the ancient duplication that made alpha and beta.

74
00:06:40.175 --> 00:06:46.950
So the gene you share with a chicken is more similar to yours than the gene sitting next to it in your own genome.

75
00:06:46.950 --> 00:06:50.800
That is not an anomaly; it is what you should expect.

76
00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:57.400
The duplication is older than the bird-mammal split, so the alpha-beta pair has had longer to diverge.

77
00:06:57.400 --> 00:06:59.825
But do not take the wrong lesson.

78
00:06:59.825 --> 00:07:07.575
It is tempting to conclude that paralogs are simply the distant ones and orthologs the close ones, and use distance to classify.

79
00:07:07.575 --> 00:07:09.650
That does not hold.

80
00:07:09.650 --> 00:07:17.175
A fast-evolving ortholog can be very distant, and a recent duplication produces paralogs that are nearly identical.

81
00:07:17.175 --> 00:07:20.500
Distance is a consequence, not a definition.

82
00:07:20.500 --> 00:07:29.200
What decides is the event at the node separating the two genes in the tree: speciation gives orthologs, duplication gives paralogs.

83
00:07:29.200 --> 00:07:31.075
You need the tree.

84
00:07:31.075 --> 00:07:42.625
This is the first of several places where the course needs the tree structure of sequences — Session eight uses it for homology-aware data splits, and Session twelve uses it for coevolution.

85
00:07:43.225 --> 00:07:48.100
A tree drawn with confident lines invites more trust than it has earned.

86
00:07:48.100 --> 00:07:51.250
Every tree-building method returns a tree.

87
00:07:51.250 --> 00:07:56.100
None of them return "I do not know", even when the data cannot decide.

88
00:07:56.100 --> 00:07:58.500
The bootstrap measures that.

89
00:07:58.500 --> 00:08:06.175
Take your alignment and resample its columns with replacement — same number of columns, but some drawn twice and some not at all.

90
00:08:06.175 --> 00:08:09.475
Rebuild the tree from the resampled alignment.

91
00:08:09.475 --> 00:08:15.175
Do that hundreds of times, and count how often each branch of your original tree reappears.

92
00:08:15.175 --> 00:08:25.150
A branch reappearing in ninety-nine per cent of the replicates is supported by many columns independently; the conclusion does not rest on a handful of positions.

93
00:08:25.150 --> 00:08:33.950
A branch appearing in forty per cent is being held up by very little, and a different sample of the same protein would have given you a different tree there.

94
00:08:33.950 --> 00:08:48.675
So a bootstrap value is the answer to "how much of this tree is the data, and how much is the method having to choose?" Published trees carry these numbers on the branches, and branches below about seventy per cent are generally not treated as resolved.

95
00:08:48.675 --> 00:08:50.750
Two honest limits.

96
00:08:50.750 --> 00:09:01.075
Real phylogenetics uses explicit models of how residues substitute, with maximum likelihood — IQ-TREE or FastTree — rather than the distance methods here.

97
00:09:01.075 --> 00:09:11.600
And the bootstrap measures consistency of the data with the method, not correctness: a systematically biased method can be confidently wrong, and resampling will not notice.

98
00:09:12.200 --> 00:09:15.150
Now to reading a multiple alignment.

99
00:09:15.150 --> 00:09:23.675
A family alignment of hundreds of sequences is far too big to read row by row, so we summarise it, and what matters is the columns.

100
00:09:23.675 --> 00:09:29.875
A column where every sequence has the same residue has been conserved through evolutionary time.

101
00:09:29.875 --> 00:09:39.225
Since evolution has had the opportunity to change it and has not, something is probably stopping it — the residue likely matters for structure or function.

102
00:09:39.225 --> 00:09:46.350
A column with many different residues has been free to change, so presumably little depends on which residue is there.

103
00:09:46.350 --> 00:09:49.525
Session one's entropy puts a number on it.

104
00:09:49.525 --> 00:09:55.725
For the residue frequencies p in a column, H equals minus the sum of p times log-two p.

105
00:09:55.725 --> 00:09:59.050
A fully conserved column gives zero bits.

106
00:09:59.050 --> 00:10:07.475
The maximum for a protein column, with all twenty amino acids equally common, is log-two twenty, about four point three two bits.

107
00:10:07.475 --> 00:10:11.825
For DNA the maximum is log-two four, which is two bits.

108
00:10:11.825 --> 00:10:23.325
Entropy measures variability, but it is usually more convenient to measure conservation, so we take the distance from the maximum: information content equals log-two twenty minus H.

109
00:10:23.325 --> 00:10:27.500
High for conserved columns, near zero for variable ones.

110
00:10:27.500 --> 00:10:29.300
Two caveats.

111
00:10:29.300 --> 00:10:36.075
This simple version assumes all twenty amino acids are equally common in the background, which they are not.

112
00:10:36.075 --> 00:10:40.775
And with few sequences a column can look conserved purely by chance.

113
00:10:40.775 --> 00:10:46.300
Real logo programs correct for both — background frequencies and small samples.

114
00:10:46.900 --> 00:10:49.850
Before I show you this, make a prediction.

115
00:10:49.850 --> 00:10:57.125
This is the Pfam globin family: seventy-three globins from bacteria, plants, worms, insects and vertebrates.

116
00:10:57.125 --> 00:11:01.400
They all bind haem, and haem binding is what globins are for.

117
00:11:01.400 --> 00:11:05.275
Which columns would you expect to be conserved across all of them?

118
00:11:05.275 --> 00:11:11.575
The intuitive answer is the ones that touch the haem — the iron-binding residues, the pocket around it.

119
00:11:11.575 --> 00:11:13.575
Hold that thought.

120
00:11:13.575 --> 00:11:15.725
Here is the logo.

121
00:11:15.725 --> 00:11:25.450
Each column is a stack of letters; the stack's height is the column's information content in bits, and each letter's height within the stack is that residue's frequency.

122
00:11:25.450 --> 00:11:32.825
A tall stack with one big letter is a conserved position; a low stack of many small letters is a variable one.

123
00:11:32.825 --> 00:11:39.600
Gaps lower the stack too, because the height is scaled by the fraction of sequences that have any residue there at all.

124
00:11:39.600 --> 00:11:46.100
The colours are chemistry: hydrophobic black, polar green, basic blue, acidic red, amide purple.

125
00:11:46.100 --> 00:11:54.275
And the three labelled columns are numbered as in mature human beta-globin, so you can map them onto a protein we have been using all session.

126
00:11:54.275 --> 00:11:58.650
The prediction was broadly right, and much narrower than you would guess.

127
00:11:58.650 --> 00:12:04.700
Across all seventy-three sequences, exactly two columns are identical in every one.

128
00:12:04.700 --> 00:12:11.350
Histidine ninety-two — the proximal histidine, F-eight — whose side chain binds the haem iron directly.

129
00:12:11.350 --> 00:12:15.925
And phenylalanine forty-two, C-D-one, which packs against the haem.

130
00:12:15.925 --> 00:12:26.500
A third, histidine sixty-three, the distal histidine on the oxygen-binding side, is histidine in seventy-three per cent of the sequences — common, but not universal.

131
00:12:26.500 --> 00:12:28.450
Two residues.

132
00:12:28.450 --> 00:12:34.625
That is what a protein family spanning bacteria to vertebrates absolutely cannot do without.

133
00:12:35.225 --> 00:12:40.350
Sit with how strange that is, because it is the most important thing in this block.

134
00:12:40.350 --> 00:12:44.875
Only two columns are identical in all seventy-three sequences.

135
00:12:44.875 --> 00:12:50.625
The average column carries one point two bits, against a maximum of four point three two.

136
00:12:50.625 --> 00:12:56.600
And most pairs of globins in this alignment share fewer than twenty per cent of their residues.

137
00:12:56.600 --> 00:13:02.300
Yet every one of them folds into the same globin fold and binds haem in the same place.

138
00:13:02.300 --> 00:13:06.075
Sequence nearly gone; structure and function kept.

139
00:13:06.075 --> 00:13:06.850
How?

140
00:13:06.850 --> 00:13:09.975
Not by identical residues, mostly.

141
00:13:09.975 --> 00:13:17.600
Look at the black stacks — leucine, valine, isoleucine, phenylalanine — often with several residues sharing one column.

142
00:13:17.600 --> 00:13:21.125
Those positions are not conserved as a residue.

143
00:13:21.125 --> 00:13:27.475
They are conserved as a property: whatever sits there must be hydrophobic, because it points into the core.

144
00:13:27.475 --> 00:13:36.525
Any of several residues will do, so no single letter dominates the column and the stack stays shortish, but the constraint is real and absolute.

145
00:13:36.525 --> 00:13:45.150
This is why substitution matrices have those positive hydrophobic blocks, and it is why similarity is a better guide than identity.

146
00:13:45.150 --> 00:13:50.875
Judge this family by identity and you would conclude the sequences are barely related.

147
00:13:50.875 --> 00:14:01.550
They are related, and the alignment can point you at the residues the protein cannot do without — two of them — and at the many positions where chemistry matters but identity does not.

148
00:14:01.550 --> 00:14:12.500
A practical note: closely related sequences are over-represented in most alignments, which skews the frequencies, so real tools weight sequences by how redundant they are.

149
00:14:12.500 --> 00:14:19.200
The lab compares a logo of five sequences with this one of seventy-three, and the difference is instructive.

150
00:14:19.800 --> 00:14:26.325
Logos are not only for families of homologs, and this example shows a different kind of thing entirely.

151
00:14:26.325 --> 00:14:42.950
Many proteins destined for secretion, a membrane, or an organelle of the secretory pathway begin with a signal peptide: a short N-terminal stretch, typically fifteen to thirty residues, that sends the new chain into the pathway and is then cut off once it has done its job.

152
00:14:42.950 --> 00:14:48.050
Signal peptides from unrelated proteins share no common ancestor.

153
00:14:48.050 --> 00:14:53.675
They cannot be aligned residue by residue, because there is nothing homologous to align.

154
00:14:53.675 --> 00:15:05.725
But they share a design, and the first collections of a few dozen sequences around nineteen eighty already showed it: a positively charged N-terminus, then a hydrophobic stretch, then a more polar end.

155
00:15:05.725 --> 00:15:09.900
Today those are the n-region, the h-region and the c-region.

156
00:15:09.900 --> 00:15:19.375
To draw that as a logo we need an alignment, and since homology will not give us one, we use the one position they all genuinely share: the cleavage site.

157
00:15:19.375 --> 00:15:23.200
Line them all up on the cut and look at the columns around it.

158
00:15:23.200 --> 00:15:28.550
The logo shows two different kinds of signal, and this is the point of the example.

159
00:15:28.550 --> 00:15:37.475
In the h-region, no column is conserved at all — but every column leans the same way, towards hydrophobic residues and above all leucine.

160
00:15:37.475 --> 00:15:42.600
What matters there is a property of a whole stretch, not a residue at a position.

161
00:15:42.600 --> 00:15:47.275
Near the cut, the opposite: two single positions stand out sharply.

162
00:15:47.275 --> 00:15:56.225
Minus three and minus one carry small uncharged residues, mostly alanine, also glycine and serine, while minus two between them is free.

163
00:15:56.225 --> 00:16:03.600
This is the minus-three, minus-one rule, found in nineteen eighty-three in a collection of seventy-eight signal peptides.

164
00:16:03.600 --> 00:16:11.325
The enzyme that cuts, signal peptidase, needs small side chains at those two positions and does not care what is between them.

165
00:16:11.325 --> 00:16:22.450
The n-region is blurred in the logo, and that is an artefact worth recognising: signal peptides differ in length, so columns at the left-hand end mix residues from different regions.

166
00:16:22.450 --> 00:16:26.975
The charge is clearest in bacteria, as lysine and arginine.

167
00:16:26.975 --> 00:16:32.125
Note also that the two logos have different y-scales — compare shapes, not heights.

168
00:16:32.125 --> 00:16:36.550
Like a family's logo, this description can be turned into a score.

169
00:16:36.550 --> 00:16:50.450
Von Heijne did exactly that in nineteen eighty-six, building a position weight matrix for positions minus thirteen to plus two around the cleavage site — which is Session five's PSSM idea, arriving twenty years early.

170
00:16:50.450 --> 00:17:05.400
The signal peptide comes back repeatedly: Session seven compares that weight matrix with a neural network, Session eight uses h-region hydrophobicity as a feature, and Session eleven lets recurrent networks find the regions by themselves.

171
00:17:06.000 --> 00:17:09.450
One last thing, which points at the rest of the course.

172
00:17:09.450 --> 00:17:21.775
Everything in this session rests on a substitution matrix, and a substitution matrix is an explicit, readable model of evolution: log-odds of substitutions, counted in related proteins.

173
00:17:21.775 --> 00:17:27.300
You can open BLOSUM sixty-two, look at a number, and say why it is what it is.

174
00:17:27.300 --> 00:17:30.250
Recent methods do not work that way.

175
00:17:30.250 --> 00:17:45.225
TM-Vec is a deep network trained to predict the structural similarity of two proteins — their TM-score — directly from their sequences, with no substitution matrix anywhere, and it finds remote homologs better than sequence alignment does.

176
00:17:45.225 --> 00:17:50.525
DeepBLAST, from the same work, produces the alignment itself from sequence alone.

177
00:17:50.525 --> 00:18:04.375
Foldseek takes another route: it describes each residue's three-dimensional environment as a letter from a twenty-letter structural alphabet, turning a structure into a string, and then aligns those strings with fast sequence-search machinery.

178
00:18:04.375 --> 00:18:17.025
The result is four to five orders of magnitude faster than structural aligners, at eighty-six, eighty-eight and a hundred and thirty-three per cent of the sensitivity of Dali, TM-align and CE respectively.

179
00:18:17.025 --> 00:18:20.250
So here is the question I want to leave with you.

180
00:18:20.250 --> 00:18:27.175
A substitution matrix is a hypothesis about evolution that you can read, argue with, and reject.

181
00:18:27.175 --> 00:18:31.775
A learned similarity works better and cannot be read the same way.

182
00:18:31.775 --> 00:18:41.000
Is it still a hypothesis about evolution, or has it become something else — a prediction that happens to be right, with no claim about history attached?

183
00:18:41.000 --> 00:18:47.725
I do not think that question has a settled answer, and it comes back in every remaining session of this course.

184
00:18:48.325 --> 00:18:51.800
To summarise the session in three sentences.

185
00:18:51.800 --> 00:19:00.675
Dynamic programming finds the optimal alignment for a given scoring scheme — guaranteed optimal, and that guarantee is real and worth something.

186
00:19:00.675 --> 00:19:07.250
The scoring scheme is a choice: a substitution matrix, a gap-opening cost, a gap-extension cost.

187
00:19:07.250 --> 00:19:15.975
Change the choice and you change the answer, which means the optimum is optimal with respect to your assumptions rather than with respect to the truth.

188
00:19:15.975 --> 00:19:21.850
And a high score still has to beat chance, which is why Session five's E-values exist.

189
00:19:21.850 --> 00:19:33.275
Beyond two sequences, a multiple alignment gives you something a pair cannot: each column becomes a distribution over amino acids, showing what the family keeps and what it lets vary.

190
00:19:33.275 --> 00:19:41.900
In the globins that was two absolutely conserved residues and a great many positions conserved as chemistry rather than as identity.

191
00:19:41.900 --> 00:19:45.725
Those column distributions are the bridge to the next session.

192
00:19:45.725 --> 00:19:49.700
Session five turns them into position-specific scores.

193
00:19:50.300 --> 00:19:52.425
That is Session four.

194
00:19:52.425 --> 00:20:03.500
The notebook implements Needleman-Wunsch and Smith-Waterman from scratch, filling a matrix one cell at a time with the reasoning printed out, and then builds the globin gene tree by neighbour joining.

195
00:20:03.500 --> 00:20:17.625
The lab has you fill a matrix by hand with BLOSUM sixty-two, see how much the answer moves when the matrix and gap penalties change, build a small multiple alignment, and work out column entropies before drawing the globin logo.

196
00:20:17.625 --> 00:20:21.875
Session five takes up the two problems this session leaves open.

197
00:20:21.875 --> 00:20:29.775
Needleman-Wunsch does not scale to searching one sequence against an entire database — that is what BLAST's heuristic is for.

198
00:20:29.775 --> 00:20:35.350
And once you have many aligned sequences rather than two, you can build a profile from them.

199
00:20:35.350 --> 00:20:36.975
See you there.

